          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-nordictrackcommercial1250treadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Model Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          NordicTrack Commercial 1250 <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Treadmill Review</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The Commercial 1250 is the cheapest way into NordicTrack’s running series, and the machine sold under that name today is not the one most older reviews describe. This review sets out the figures NordicTrack and two US retailers confirm for the current model, what they mean in use, and when the 1750 or a T Series machine is the better buy.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#current-specs" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Current Specs
          </a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Who Should Buy It
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/" rel="author noopener" target="_blank" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Author and lab tester</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 14, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          14 October 2026 we read NordicTrack's US product page for the Commercial 1250 (SKU
          NTL14125), including its specification block, warranty text, FAQ and footnotes, and
          cross-checked it against P.C. Richard &amp; Son's and Nebraska Furniture Mart's listings
          for the same model number, plus independent reviews from Treadmill Review Guru (updated
          March 2025) and BarBend (updated December 2024). NordicTrack and P.C. Richard agree on the
          3.6 CHP motor rating, the 22 x 60 in. belt, the 400 lb maximum user weight, 0-12 mph, the
          77.3 x 37 x 59.5 in. footprint and two years of parts and one year of labour cover;
          NordicTrack and Nebraska Furniture Mart agree on the 10 in. tilting screen, the -3% to 12%
          incline range, the 400 lb rating and the footprint. BarBend's review describes the earlier
          machine (3.0 CHP, 20 x 60 in., 300 lb), and we treat it as such. Figures that appear in a
          single source are labelled. We have not tested the Commercial 1250, and none of these
          numbers are our own measurements. Prices are those shown on 14 October 2026 and change
          constantly. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon link on this page; it does
          not change what we say about the machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. NordicTrack Commercial 1250 Treadmill: The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#which-1250" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Which Commercial 1250? Why Older Reviews Disagree</a>
          <a href="#current-specs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The Specifications NordicTrack Confirms</a>
          <a href="#motor-and-warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The 3.6 CHP Motor and the Two-Year Motor Warranty</a>
          <a href="#deck" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Deck, Cushioning and the 400 lb Rating</a>
          <a href="#incline-speed-screen" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Incline, Speed and the 10-Inch Screen</a>
          <a href="#folding-and-space" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Folding, Footprint and Where It Fits</a>
          <a href="#warranty-delivery" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Warranty, Delivery and Returns</a>
          <a href="#what-reviewers-report" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. What Independent Reviewers Consistently Report</a>
          <a href="#vs-alternatives" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Commercial 1250 vs the 1750, the T Series and Others</a>
          <a href="#price-and-timing" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Price, Sales and When to Buy</a>
          <a href="#ownership" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Owning One: Resets, Maintenance and Faults</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">13. Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Not</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">14. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Look up the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 treadmill and you will find it described with a
          3.0 or a 3.6 CHP motor, a belt 20 or 22 inches wide, and a weight limit of 300 or 400 lb.
          Both sets of figures have been true of a machine sold under the name. This page is about
          the one on sale now, model number NTL14125, using the figures NordicTrack publishes for it
          and that P.C. Richard &amp; Son and Nebraska Furniture Mart repeat for the same model
          number. Where the older figures still circulate, we say where they came from rather than
          mixing them in.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. NordicTrack Commercial 1250 Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The NordicTrack Commercial 1250 treadmill is the cheapest machine in NordicTrack's
            running series and, on the current model's figures, a full-size one: a 22 by 60-inch
            folding deck, decline to -3% and incline to 12%, 12 mph and a 400 lb rating, for about
            $1,600 on the discounts that were running when we checked.</strong> What it gives up
            against the Commercial 1750 above it is a larger pivoting screen and, more importantly,
            the lifetime motor warranty. Its motor is covered for two years.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four facts carry most of the decision. The belt is 22 inches wide and 60 long on the
            current model, the same as the 1750, which is room for a full running stride and some
            margin either side. The incline runs from -3% to 12%, identical to the 1750 and 2450.
            The 400 lb maximum user weight is also the same as the 1750's current figure. And the
            motor warranty is two years, which is the single clearest difference between the 1250
            and the machines above it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read every figure with the model number in mind. Reviews written before 2025 describe a
            Commercial 1250 with a 3.0 CHP motor, a 20-inch belt and a 300 lb limit. The figures on
            this page are for NTL14125, the model NordicTrack sells now. We have not tested it; what
            follows is what the published figures and independent coverage imply. If the choice you
            are really making is between the 1250 and the 1750, our
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1250 vs 1750 comparison</a>
            covers that decision on its own, and our
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1750 review</a>
            covers the machine above.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
      <div class="not-prose">
        {
          PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-3">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-white font-black text-xl leading-tight m-0">
                      {index + 1}. {product.name}
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4 flex-wrap">
                    <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Typical street price</span>
                    <span class="ml-auto font-black text-gray-900">{product.priceRange}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400 mb-4">{product.specs}</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6" set:html={product.description} />
                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mt-0 mb-2">What We Like</h4>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{pro}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mt-0 mb-2">What We Don't Like</h4>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{con}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="border-t border-gray-100 pt-4">
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold mb-4">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                      <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
                        {product.amazonLabel} &rarr;
                      </a>
                      {product.reviewUrl && (
                        <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline text-sm hover:underline">
                          Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="which-1250" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Which Commercial 1250? Why Older Reviews Disagree</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack keeps model names while changing the hardware underneath. The Commercial 1750
            is the best-known case, and the 1250 has been through the same thing. That does not make
            older reviews wrong about the machines they describe. It does mean a review, a forum post
            or a clearance listing can be accurate about one Commercial 1250 and misleading about the
            one you would buy today.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the earlier version looked like</h3>
          <p>
            BarBend's review, last updated in December 2024, describes a Commercial 1250 with a
            3.0 CHP motor, a 20 by 60-inch belt, 1.9-inch rollers and a 300 lb weight capacity, with
            the same 10-inch tilting screen, 12 mph and -3% to 12% range. Treadmill Review Guru's
            review, updated in March 2025, describes the newer machine and says in terms that the
            motor, the deck and the weight capacity were all increased over the previous model. Garage
            Gym Reviews describes the 1250 as having been discontinued for a time and then brought
            back. Put together, the picture is of an earlier 1250 and a revised one that share a name,
            a screen and a speed range but not a motor rating, a belt width or a capacity.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What NordicTrack lists now</h3>
          <p>
            On 14 October 2026 NordicTrack's own page for the Commercial 1250, model NTL14125, gave a
            3.6 CHP motor, a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 400 lb maximum user weight, 0 to 12 mph and
            incline from -3% to 12%. P.C. Richard &amp; Son's listing for NTL14125 repeated the motor,
            belt, capacity and speed, and Nebraska Furniture Mart's listing for the same model number
            repeated the capacity, the screen, the incline range and the footprint. That agreement on a
            single model number is why this page states figures that our earlier
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">1250 vs 1750 comparison</a>
            and our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best NordicTrack treadmills</a>
            roundup treated with caution; at the time those were written, NordicTrack's page did not
            state the 1250's motor rating or belt width.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to make sure you are buying the current one</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Read the model number, not the name.</strong> Look for NTL14125 in the listing's specification block. On a showroom or second-hand machine, read it from the label on the frame.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not rely on the screen to tell them apart.</strong> Both versions have a 10-inch tilting touchscreen, so unlike the 1750, the screen size will not identify an older unit.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the belt width and the capacity.</strong> A listing or a unit with a 20-inch belt or a 300 lb limit describes the earlier machine, whatever the title says.</li>
            <li><strong>Get decisive figures in writing.</strong> A heavier user or a broad-shouldered runner should ask the seller to confirm the capacity and belt width for the exact unit before paying.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="current-specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Specifications NordicTrack Confirms</h2>
<p>
            The table separates the figures NordicTrack publishes and an independent retailer listing
            repeats for the same model number from the ones that appear in only one place. Everything
            was read on 14 October 2026.
          </p>
