              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">309.3 lb boxed (NordicTrack); 308 lb (Nebraska Furniture Mart, unlabelled); 322 lb (Treadmill Review Guru)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sources differ; plan for about 310 lb in the box</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            One small wrinkle: P.C. Richard's structured specification field gives the incline as "0 to
            12 percent", while the product description on the same page gives "12% incline to -3%
            decline". NordicTrack and Nebraska Furniture Mart both give -3% to 12%, and so do every
            independent review we read, so we treat the structured field as an omission rather than a
            conflict. NordicTrack also lists RunFlex cushioning, an AutoBreeze fan that changes speed
            with the workout, two 2-inch speakers, a USB-C charging port, Bluetooth for headphones and
            heart-rate monitors, and the iFIT features it calls SmartAdjust and ActivePulse. Those are
            features rather than measurements, and how useful they are depends on whether you keep an
            iFIT membership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-and-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The 3.6 CHP Motor and the Two-Year Motor Warranty</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack rates the current Commercial 1250's drive motor at 3.6 continuous horsepower,
            and P.C. Richard's listing for NTL14125 describes a "3.6 CHP drive system". That is the
            same rating NordicTrack gives the T Series 16, and below the 4.25 CHP it gives the
            Commercial 1750 and 2450. Earlier 1250s were reviewed at 3.0 CHP.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What NordicTrack's footnote says</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack footnotes the figure. Its footnote says CHP is a term the treadmill industry
            uses for consumer comparison, that the rating is measured in laboratory testing with a
            dynamometer, and that it does not denote the operational horsepower of the treadmill in
            ordinary household use, where the motor does not operate at the horsepower shown. The
            practical point is that a CHP figure helps you compare machines rated the same way and
            says much less about how a motor will feel under you. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a>
            explains peak, continuous and test ratings and what each does and does not tell you.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the warranty matters more than the rating</h3>
          <p>
            What NordicTrack commits to in writing is two years on the 1250's motor. It covers the
            1750's and 2450's motors for life. A manufacturer sets warranty terms with its own failure
            data in front of it, so the gap tells you where NordicTrack expects its costs to land.
            NordicTrack's own FAQ on the 1250 page frames the difference between the 1250 and 1750 as
            "screen size, motor size and motor warranty", and tells buyers to choose the 1250 if they
            "don't mind the smaller screen size or warranty limitations".
          </p>
          <p>
            Two points keep it in proportion. On a well-kept home treadmill the motor is rarely the
            first thing to fail; belts, decks and control boards usually go first, and those fall under
            the two-year parts term on every Commercial model alike. And a lifetime motor warranty
            covers the part, not the labour to fit it, which is one year on all three machines. The
            two-year motor term is a real difference for a heavy user, a household sharing one machine
            or anyone who plans to keep the treadmill for a decade. For a walker or a three-runs-a-week
            jogger it is a smaller risk than it sounds. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read frame, motor, parts and labour terms line by line.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What looks after the motor</h3>
          <p>
            The things that load a treadmill motor hardest are long sessions, heavier users, steep
            incline and sustained running. Keeping the deck lubricated and the belt correctly tensioned
            does more for motor life than a quarter of a horsepower on the label. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>
            covers the routine, and the manual is the authority on the interval for your machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Deck, Cushioning and the 400 lb Rating</h2>
<p>
            The current 1250 has a 22 by 60-inch belt, the same size NordicTrack lists for the 1750 and
            2450. Sixty inches is the length running guidance generally settles on for adults who intend
            to run rather than jog, because a full stride needs room in front and behind. Twenty-two
            inches is two more than the common home-treadmill width and two more than the T Series,
            which matters most for taller runners, broad builds and anyone whose stride drifts sideways
            late in a session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Belt width on a specification sheet and the width you can actually run on are not always the
            same, because side rails can overlap the edge of the belt. If width is the reason you are
            buying, stand on one in a showroom or ask the seller about usable width between the rails.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            works through which lengths and widths suit which heights and paces.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">RunFlex cushioning</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack describes the deck's RunFlex cushioning as designed to absorb impact and provide
            stability, and publishes no number for it. Treadmill Review Guru found the cushioning softer
            than most competitors'. Cushioning cannot be compared from a listing; it is something you
            judge by standing on it. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioning explainer</a>
            covers what a cushioned deck does and does not do for your joints. In short, it softens the
            landing compared with a road, which most people find more comfortable, but it is not a
            treatment for an injury.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 400 lb maximum user weight</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack lists the current model at 400 lb (182 kg), and P.C. Richard and Nebraska
            Furniture Mart agree for NTL14125. That is 75 lb more than the T Series and 100 lb more than
            the earlier 1250 was reviewed at. A rating is a ceiling, not a target; a machine used near
            its limit every day works harder than one with margin, and with a two-year motor warranty a
            heavier user has more reason than most to keep that margin. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-happens-if-you-exceed-treadmill-weight-limit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what happens if you exceed a treadmill's weight limit</a>
            explains why it matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-speed-screen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Incline, Speed and the 10-Inch Screen</h2>
<p>
            The 1250 inclines to 12%, declines to -3% and runs from 0 to 12 mph. Those ranges are
            identical to the Commercial 1750 on NordicTrack's current listings, and the 2450 differs only
            in going to 14 mph. Nothing you pay between the 1250 and the 1750 buys more climbing or more
            speed.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline and decline</h3>
          <p>
            Twelve percent is a percent grade, a little under seven degrees, which is plenty for
            incline walking, the <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            at its full setting, and most hill intervals. If steep incline walking is the main reason you
            are buying and you want to go well beyond 12%, an incline trainer is the better tool; our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a>
            guide covers them. The -3% decline lets iFIT routes include descents and gives runners
            training for a hilly race a way to practise them. It is a reason to choose a Commercial model
            over the T Series, which has none.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Twelve miles an hour</h3>
          <p>
            A 12 mph ceiling is a five-minute mile. For steady running and most intervals it will never be
            the limit. If you do fast repeats above it, the Commercial 2450 is the NordicTrack that goes to
            14 mph. Our <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">interval workouts guide</a>
            covers how to structure sessions within a machine's range.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The screen, and what needs a membership</h3>
          <p>
            The 1250's 10-inch HD touchscreen tilts to adjust the viewing angle. It does not rotate, which
            is the main practical difference from the 1750's 16-inch pivoting screen: on the 1750 you can
            turn the screen to face the room for iFIT's strength and yoga classes. NordicTrack's FAQ says
            the core connected features, including SmartAdjust and terrain-matching incline, are the same
            on the 1250 as on the larger-screened models, with a paid iFIT membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            Without a paid membership, NordicTrack says the treadmill operates in manual mode and will not
            include iFIT-enabled features such as SmartAdjust or ActivePulse. A monthly membership's first
            month is included with a purchase from NordicTrack. We do not quote the membership price
            because it changes; check it on iFIT's own site and multiply it by the years you expect to
            keep the machine. Our assessment of
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a>
            sets out that arithmetic, and our guide to
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a>
            covers what still works when you stop paying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folding, Footprint and Where It Fits</h2>
<p>
            Assembled, the 1250 measures 77.3 inches long, 37 wide and 59.5 tall on NordicTrack's
            figures, which P.C. Richard and Nebraska Furniture Mart repeat. That is about 19.9 square
            feet of floor for the machine alone, the same footprint as the current 1750. It folds on the
            SpaceSaver frame, the deck lifting with EasyLift assist and the machine rolling away on
            wheels. NordicTrack does not give folded dimensions, so measure the storage space against the
            machine on delivery rather than assuming a figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Folding saves floor; it does not make the machine light. NordicTrack gives the boxed weight as
            309.3 lb. Independent reviewers consistently describe the 1250 as heavy and bulky: BarBend
            scored its portability low because of its small wheels and size, Treadmill Review Guru
            advises giving it a dedicated spot, and several owner reviews on P.C. Richard's listing say
            assembly needed a second person. Plan the delivery route, the stairs
            and the final position before it arrives.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ceiling height</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack lists a 10-inch step-up height, which we did not find confirmed elsewhere. A
            common working rule is to allow your height, plus the step-up height, plus several inches for
            the bounce of a running stride and the deck rising at full incline. For a 6 ft runner
            (72 inches) that comes to around 88 to 92 inches, which leaves little margin under a standard
            8-foot ceiling and none in many basements. The manual's guidance for your unit takes
            precedence. Measure the lowest point above where you will stand, such as a beam or a light,
            not the middle of the room.
          </p>
          <p>
            You also need clear space behind the deck and at the sides; follow the manual. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space guide</a>
            gives typical clearances, and
            <a href="/where-to-put-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to put a treadmill</a>
            covers floors, power and the footstrike noise that travels into rooms below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Warranty, Delivery and Returns</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack's manufacturer warranty on the current 1250 is ten years on the frame, two years on
            the motor, two years on parts, one year on the touchscreen and one year of labour and repairs,
            with paid extensions offered at checkout. P.C. Richard's listing repeats 24 months of parts and
            12 months of labour.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reading the split</h3>
          <p>
            The frame cover is long; the rest is short, and it is the short terms that decide what
            ownership costs from year two. The touchscreen is covered for one year, and it is expensive and
            central to how the machine is sold. Labour for one year means that from the second year a
            covered part may arrive free while the visit to fit it does not. If you are weighing an
            extended plan, price it against a technician's call-out where you live. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>
            gives typical costs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery and assembly</h3>
          <p>
            On 14 October 2026 NordicTrack offered free curbside shipping in the continental US, which it
            valued at up to $249, with in-home delivery and assembly as a paid upgrade. Retailers set their
            own delivery terms, so check them at checkout. If you will assemble it yourself, read our
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill assembly guide</a>
            first; the deck and motor hood are the heavy parts, and a second person is not optional.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 30-day trial and its return fee</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack offers a 30-day in-home trial. Its terms on the day we checked refunded the purchase
            price less a $249.99 return charge, and delivery and assembly costs were not refundable. Treat
            the trial as a safety net rather than a free test drive, and stand on a 1250 in a store first
            if you can. Third-party sellers have their own return terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buying used</h3>
          <p>
            Many treadmill warranties apply only to the original purchaser, so do not assume any cover
            passes to you; ask NordicTrack with the serial number. Read the model number and check the
            belt width, because an earlier 1250 looks almost identical from the console. Run it through
            the full incline and decline range, and check the belt and deck for wear. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            runs through the full inspection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-reviewers-report" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Independent Reviewers Consistently Report</h2>
<p>
            We include a point here only when it appears in more than one independent source. Some
            published coverage describes the earlier 3.0 CHP machine, so we have kept to points that do
            not depend on which version was reviewed.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Much of the value is tied to iFIT.</strong> BarBend, Treadmill Review Guru and Garage Gym Reviews all describe the subscription as central to the machine's appeal and its main ongoing cost, and Treadmill Review Guru notes how little the console offers without it.</li>
            <li><strong>The missing lifetime motor warranty is the main trade-off.</strong> Garage Gym Reviews and Treadmill Review Guru both single out the two-year motor cover as the thing you give up against the 1750 and 2450.</li>
            <li><strong>It is stable on the belt.</strong> BarBend's testers reported no shaking at speed, and owner reviews on P.C. Richard's listing describe it as steady, including at running pace. Owner reviews on retailer sites are often incentivised, and both retailers label them as such, so weigh them lightly.</li>
            <li><strong>It is large and heavy.</strong> BarBend rated its portability low and Treadmill Review Guru recommends a dedicated spot rather than folding it away daily. That matches the footprint and the boxed weight above.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What we have not seen consistently is a specific mechanical weakness of the current model. It
            has not been on sale long enough for long-term reports to build up, and the parts most likely
            to need attention on any connected treadmill are the belt, the deck and the electronics. Our
            guide to <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a>
            explains how to tell a pattern in owner reports from a handful of loud ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on safety records: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
            listings on 14 October 2026 and found no recall of the Commercial 1250 or of any current
            NordicTrack treadmill. iFIT's recent NordicTrack recalls covered a rowing machine (October
            2025) and adjustable dumbbells (February 2024), not treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Commercial 1250 vs the 1750, the T Series and Others</h2>
<p>
            Most buyers who look at the 1250 are choosing between it and one neighbour. Here is what
            separates them on the figures NordicTrack publishes now.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Against the Commercial 1750</h3>
          <p>
            On the current listings the belt (22 x 60 in.), the capacity (400 lb), the speed (12 mph),
            the incline range (-3% to 12%) and the footprint are identical. The differences are the screen
            (10 in. tilting against 16 in. pivoting), the motor rating (3.6 against 4.25 CHP), the motor
            warranty (two years against lifetime) and the price, which was $400 apart at NordicTrack on the
            day we checked. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">1250 vs 1750 comparison</a>
            works through that choice in detail. The rule of thumb: pay for the 1750 if you will use the
            pivoting screen for floor classes, run hard and often, or want lifetime motor cover;
            otherwise the 1250 does the same job on the belt.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Against the T Series</h3>
          <p>
            The T Series 10 and 16 are NordicTrack's walking and light-running tier: a 20 by 60-inch belt,
            incline to 12% with no decline, 12 mph, a 325 lb rating, a lower 8.3-inch step-up and two years
            on the motor. On the day we checked, NordicTrack listed the T Series 10 at the same $1,599.99 as
            the 1250, and at that price the 1250's wider belt, decline and higher rating make it the more
            capable machine for the money; the T Series makes more sense when it is discounted well below
            the 1250, or for a walker who values the lower step-up. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack T Series review</a>
            and <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-vs-commercial/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T Series vs Commercial guide</a>
            cover that choice.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Outside NordicTrack</h3>
          <p>
            If you know you will never subscribe to iFIT, a machine that works fully without a membership
            may be the better use of the money. The
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6</a>
            and <a href="/bowflex-t9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T9</a>
            both work without a subscription and carry longer motor cover. ProForm's
            <a href="/proform-carbon-pro-9000-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Carbon Pro 9000</a>
            is the sister brand's nearest equivalent, with a 16-inch screen but a 20-inch belt and no
            decline. Our <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best NordicTrack treadmills</a>
            roundup places the 1250 in the whole current range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-and-timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Price, Sales and When to Buy</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack's list price for the 1250 was $1,999.99 on 14 October 2026, and its selling price
            was $1,599.99. P.C. Richard and Nebraska Furniture Mart were both at $1,599. NordicTrack runs
            promotions for most of the year, so the list price is rarely what anyone pays, and the gap to
            the 1750 moves with whichever machine is being promoted that week.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sale events such as Black Friday tend to bring the deepest cuts across the Commercial series,
            and sometimes bundle accessories or membership time rather than cutting the price. We cannot
            say what the 1250 will cost on any future date. What we can say is how to judge a deal when you
            see one: compare it with the $1,599 seen on 14 October 2026, compare it with the 1750's price on
            the same day, and check whether the offer includes delivery, assembly or a return fee you would
            otherwise pay. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill guide</a>
            and <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy</a>
            explain when discounts tend to land and how to tell a real one from a reframed list price.
          </p>
          <p>
            One caution specific to this model: when a retailer clears stock at a price well below the
            current one, check the model number and belt width before buying. A deep discount on an earlier
            1250 buys a narrower belt, a lower capacity and a smaller motor rating than the figures on this
            page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Owning One: Resets, Maintenance and Faults</h2>
<p>
            A connected treadmill asks a little more of its owner than a basic one, mostly because of the
            software. Three habits avoid most problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know how to reset it.</strong> A frozen screen, an unresponsive console or a machine
            that will not start after an update are the faults NordicTrack owners most often search for, and
            many clear with a restart. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">resetting a NordicTrack treadmill</a>
            goes through the sequence and what to try before calling support.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the deck lubricated and the belt adjusted.</strong> A dry deck increases friction,
            which loads the motor and wears the belt, and with two years of motor cover that matters. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance guide</a>
            sets out the owner's schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Register it and keep records.</strong> Register the warranty, keep the order
            confirmation and keep a note of lubrication and adjustments. With one year of labour and two of
            parts and motor, a well-documented claim in that window is worth having.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before servicing anything, unplug the machine and remove the safety key. If you notice a
            burning smell, a belt that will not stop, sparking or a tripping breaker, stop using it and
            contact NordicTrack; our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            covers the common faults.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Not</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the Commercial 1250 if you will run as well as walk, want a full 22 by 60-inch
            folding deck and decline, and would rather keep a few hundred dollars than pay for a bigger
            screen.</strong> On the current model's figures it does everything the 1750 does on the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavier user who needs more than the T Series' 325 lb.</strong>
            The current 1250 is rated to 400 lb, and it is the cheapest NordicTrack with that rating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose the 1750 if you will run hard and often, or share the machine,</strong> and
            value lifetime motor cover, or if you will use the pivoting screen for floor classes. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">1750 review</a>
            sets out that case.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if you will never subscribe.</strong> The treadmill works in manual
            mode, but much of what you pay for in the screen and software sits idle without a membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever you decide, buy by model number.</strong> Confirm NTL14125 on the listing,
            check the belt width and the capacity, and get any figure you are relying on in writing. The
            figures that circulate for the 1250 describe more than one machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">14. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most buyers who will run as well as walk, yes, at the discounted prices NordicTrack runs most of the year. The current model (NTL14125) has a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 400 lb rating, -3% to 12% incline and 12 mph. It is worth less if you will not keep an iFIT membership, and its motor warranty is two years. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the NordicTrack Commercial 1250?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack lists the current model, NTL14125, at a 400 lb (182 kg) maximum user weight, and P.C. Richard and Nebraska Furniture Mart agree. Earlier machines sold under the same name were reviewed at 300 lb, so check the model number on a used or clearance unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack rates the current model’s motor at 3.6 CHP and covers it for two years. Its footnote says the figure is a laboratory dynamometer rating that does not denote operational horsepower in household use. Earlier 1250s were reviewed at 3.0 CHP.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 belt 20 or 22 inches wide?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The current model, NTL14125, has a 22 by 60-inch belt on NordicTrack’s listing and P.C. Richard’s listing for the same model. Reviews of the earlier machine describe a 20 by 60-inch belt. Because both versions have the same 10-inch screen, the belt width and the model number are the way to tell them apart.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 and 1750?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On NordicTrack’s current listings the belt, capacity, speed, incline range and footprint are the same. The 1750 adds a 16-inch pivoting screen in place of a 10-inch tilting one, a 4.25 CHP rating against 3.6, and a lifetime motor warranty against two years. It was $400 more at NordicTrack on 14 October 2026.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 have decline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. NordicTrack lists incline from -3% to 12% on the current model. With a paid iFIT membership both adjust automatically during workouts; without one you set them manually.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Commercial 1250</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most bad purchases of this machine come from reading the wrong version’s figures or underestimating the short warranty terms.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a review of the earlier model</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Coverage from 2024 and earlier describes a 1250 with a 3.0 CHP motor, a 20-inch belt and a 300 lb limit. Those figures may have been right for that machine. Check the model number — NTL14125 is the current one — and check the belt width, because the screen is the same on both.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cleared older unit as if it were current</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A deep discount on old stock can look like a bargain against the current price. If the unit has the narrower belt and lower capacity, it is a different machine, and the comparison with today’s 1250 does not hold.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the 30-day trial as free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">NordicTrack’s in-home trial deducted a $249.99 return charge when we checked, and delivery and assembly costs were not refundable. Measure the room and the ceiling first, and read the return terms of whichever seller you use.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting how short most of the warranty is</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Only the frame is covered for ten years. The motor and parts are covered for two, the touchscreen and labour for one. From year two a failed screen or a technician’s visit is likely to be on you, so price that before deciding whether an extended plan is worth it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

